    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d570">

                <pb facs="0634" n="530" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg530"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/4600: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Switzerland (Wilson), at Geneva</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-03-08T12:00:00-05:00">March 8, 1932—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>35. Your 58, March 7, 2 p.m. I feel that in such conversations with
                    representatives of other states you should proceed guardedly as regards
                    initiative. In particular, you should guard against giving any impression that
                    the American Government is taking sides as between China and Japan. Our position
                    is based on our view of treaty rights and obligations, and we must not become
                    protagonists of the views, interests or activities, as such, of either of the
                        disputants.<anchor n="65" xml:id="d570-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d570-f1" n="65" xml:id="d570-f1-content">
                <p>The Minister replied in telegram No. 66, March 9, 4 p.m.: “All members of the
                    Delegation have made and will continue to make the idea you lay down our guiding
                    principle.” (793.94/4641)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
